  "event_exposure": {
    "note": "Event taxonomy, asset-event exposure and regime effects (docs/EVENT_AWARENESS.md §2). importance and expected_volatility are configured priors (0-100), never measurements. Exposure levels map to weights through 'levels'; '$SELF' is the event's own symbol (an earnings company, an option-expiry currency). Scenario directions come from the causal graph when a scenario names a trigger; 'expected' in a scenario is a configured direction (basis 'configured template') used only where no causal rule exists. regime_effects: first cause whose regimes are all active wins; the number is the expected sign of the symbol's own quoted value (a yield symbol at -1 means Treasuries rally). 1.1.0 (docs/EVENT_AWARENESS.md §8): 'kind' per class (DECISION, PRESS_CONFERENCE, MINUTES, SPEECH, TESTIMONY, PROJECTIONS, RELEASE, AUCTION, EARNINGS, EXPIRY, STATEMENT, OTHER); speaker roles from the source title (speaker_roles, Vice Chair before Chair) move a speech or testimony to its speaker's class (speaker_classes), so only the Chair uses the Chair-level classes; speeches and testimonies of one class on one day stay separate events (group_by_name). 1.2.0 (event-risk audit 2 Oct): a scenario gives no direction to an asset whose competing causal paths are conflicting and lists it as unresolved (EVR-1); option expiries are scored by tenor (expiry_tenor_importance, EVR-8); the labour template marks the unemployment rate as an inverted headline metric (surprise_polarity, EVR-9); the SANCTIONS rule no longer matches on the bare words 'sanctions' / 'sanctioned', and an event matched without a causal trigger exposes only the assets it was itself mapped to (EVR-6).",
